
export const medicinePrompts = [

    // --- CLINICAL DOCUMENTATION (1-12) ---
    {"id":"med-1","title":"SOAP Note Writing Framework","prompt":"You are a medical educator teaching clinical documentation to third-year medical students at a teaching hospital. Write a comprehensive guide to writing high-quality SOAP notes, using a fictional case as a teaching example: a 58-year-old male presenting with 3 days of progressively worsening shortness of breath, productive cough with yellow sputum, fever of 38.9°C, and oxygen saturation of 93% on room air, with a history of COPD and type 2 diabetes. For each SOAP section, explain what belongs there conceptually, what common student mistakes to avoid, and then write an exemplary version for this case: Subjective (chief complaint, HPI using OLDCARTS framework, relevant PMH/PSH/medications/allergies/social history/family history/ROS), Objective (vital signs, physical exam findings organized by system), Assessment (primary diagnosis with reasoning, two differential diagnoses), Plan (organized by problem with specific orders, medications with doses, patient education, follow-up). For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-2","title":"H&P Note Masterclass","prompt":"You are a clinical educator writing a teaching guide for medical students on how to write a complete History and Physical (H&P) note. Use a fictional case of a 45-year-old woman presenting with two weeks of fatigue, exertional dyspnea, and pallor. Walk through every section in order: Chief Complaint, History of Present Illness (with OPQRST mnemonic applied), Past Medical History, Past Surgical History, Family History, Social History (including smoking, alcohol, occupation, living situation), Allergies, Current Medications (with doses and indications), Review of Systems (at least 10 systems), and Physical Examination (general appearance, vitals, HEENT, neck, cardiovascular, pulmonary, abdomen, extremities, neurological, skin). After the note, add a teaching commentary explaining three documentation pitfalls students commonly make with H&P notes and how to avoid them. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-3","title":"Discharge Summary Writing Guide","prompt":"You are a hospitalist physician educator writing a guide for interns on how to write a clear, complete, and safe hospital discharge summary. Use a fictional case: a 72-year-old woman admitted for acute decompensated heart failure who is now ready for discharge after a 4-day admission. Write an exemplary discharge summary that includes: admission diagnosis, hospital course narrative, procedures performed, key lab and imaging results, discharge condition, discharge diagnosis, discharge medications (with changes from admission clearly flagged), follow-up plan with specific appointments and labs, pending results that need follow-up, and patient education provided. After the sample document, write a section titled 'Teaching Points' that explains why each component matters for patient safety during care transitions. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","discharge-planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-4","title":"Progress Note Writing for Residents","prompt":"You are an attending physician conducting a documentation workshop for first-year residents. Explain the difference between a full admission note and a daily progress note, then write two exemplary progress notes — one for a medical patient (fictional 60-year-old male, day 2 of community-acquired pneumonia treatment) and one for a surgical patient (fictional 55-year-old female, post-operative day 1 after laparoscopic cholecystectomy). For each note, use the appropriate SOAP or SBAR structure, include assessment and plan organized by active problem list, and annotate each section with brief explanations of what the attending looks for. End with five common progress note mistakes that get flagged during chart audits and how to correct them. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","residency-training"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-5","title":"Procedure Note Template and Teaching Guide","prompt":"You are a proceduralist educator teaching medical students and residents how to write accurate, complete procedure notes. Write a comprehensive teaching guide covering the essential elements of a procedure note: date and time, procedure performed, indication, informed consent obtained (and patient understanding confirmed), performing physician and assistants, patient position and preparation, anesthesia or sedation used, technique description in chronological order, specimens sent, estimated blood loss, complications encountered, patient tolerance, and post-procedure plan. Then write two sample procedure notes using fictional cases: (1) a lumbar puncture performed on a 28-year-old for suspected meningitis evaluation, and (2) a central venous catheter placement in a 65-year-old in the ICU. After each note, highlight one teaching point specific to that procedure. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","procedures"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-6","title":"EHR Documentation Best Practices","prompt":"You are a clinical informatics educator writing a training module for physicians transitioning to a new electronic health record system. Write a comprehensive guide titled 'Documentation Excellence in the EHR' that covers: the purpose of clinical documentation (communication, continuity, legal record, billing), the problem with copy-paste and note bloat (with a before-and-after example), writing notes that are concise yet complete, using problem-oriented charting effectively, avoiding over-documentation in routine visits, the importance of updating the medication list and problem list at each encounter, and how to write an attestation for resident notes. Include a section on common EHR documentation errors that lead to adverse events or billing compliance issues. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","ehr","clinical-documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:25:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-7","title":"Operative Report Writing Guide","prompt":"You are a surgical educator teaching senior residents how to dictate and write operative reports. Write a detailed guide covering the required elements of an operative report: preoperative diagnosis, postoperative diagnosis, procedure performed, surgeon and assistants, anesthesia type, indications for surgery, description of findings, detailed technique (step by step in past tense, active voice), complications, specimens sent to pathology, estimated blood loss, fluids administered, counts correct confirmation, patient condition at end of procedure, and disposition. Then write a sample operative report for a fictional laparoscopic appendectomy case in an 18-year-old with acute appendicitis. Annotate the report to explain why specific language matters (e.g., 'the appendix appeared grossly inflamed with no perforation noted' vs. vague descriptions). For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","surgery","clinical-documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-8","title":"Referral Letter Writing Framework","prompt":"You are a primary care physician educator teaching medical students how to write effective specialist referral letters. Explain why a well-written referral letter matters for patient safety and care continuity, then write a step-by-step framework covering: opening with a clear referral request and urgency level, patient demographics, reason for referral (specific clinical question to answer), relevant history and physical findings, current medications and allergies, pertinent labs and imaging with dates, what has already been tried, patient's insurance and contact information, and a professional closing. Then write two sample referral letters using fictional cases: (1) a primary care referral to cardiology for a 62-year-old with exertional chest pain, and (2) a pediatrician referral to neurology for a 6-year-old with new-onset seizures. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-9","title":"Consent Documentation in Clinical Practice","prompt":"You are a medical ethics and legal educator teaching residents about informed consent documentation. Write a comprehensive guide covering: what informed consent is legally and ethically (not just a signature), the elements that must be communicated to the patient (diagnosis, proposed procedure, alternatives including no treatment, material risks, expected benefits, who will perform it), assessing decision-making capacity, documentation of the consent conversation in the medical record, special situations (emergency consent, surrogate decision-makers, minors, patients with cognitive impairment), and how to handle a patient who refuses a recommended treatment. Include a sample consent conversation note for a fictional patient agreeing to coronary angiography, and one documenting a patient's informed refusal of a blood transfusion on religious grounds. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","ethics","clinical-documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-10","title":"Radiology Report Interpretation for Clinicians","prompt":"You are a radiology educator writing a guide for medical students and residents on how to read and interpret radiology reports in clinical practice. Cover the standard structure of a radiology report (clinical indication, technique, comparison, findings, impression), how to identify the most clinically actionable information, understanding common radiology terminology (hyperdense, lucent, consolidation, ground-glass opacity, effusion, mass effect), how to communicate with radiologists effectively when you have clinical questions, how to document imaging findings in your own notes accurately without misrepresenting the radiologist's interpretation, and how to handle incidental findings. Use fictional examples for a chest CT report with a pulmonary nodule and an MRI brain report with a white matter lesion. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","radiology","clinical-documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-11","title":"Medication Reconciliation Documentation","prompt":"You are a patient safety educator teaching pharmacy students and nurses about medication reconciliation as a critical safety practice. Write a comprehensive educational guide covering: why medication reconciliation errors are a leading cause of preventable harm, the three required reconciliation points (admission, transfer, discharge), how to conduct a thorough medication history interview, sources to verify the medication list (patient, pharmacy, PCP, pill bottles), what to do when sources conflict, how to document the reconciliation process clearly in the EHR, common high-risk medications that require special attention during reconciliation (anticoagulants, insulin, opioids, antiepileptics), and how to counsel patients on medication changes at discharge. Include a sample reconciliation scenario using a fictional patient on 8 medications being admitted for elective surgery. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pharmacy","patient-safety","clinical-documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-12","title":"Clinical Note Review and Feedback","prompt":"You are a clinical documentation expert and medical educator. Review the following clinical note and provide structured educational feedback. For each section (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan), identify: (1) what is done well, (2) what critical information is missing, (3) what could be more concise or clearly expressed, and (4) any documentation that could create a safety risk or billing compliance issue. After your critique, rewrite the note showing the improved version with annotations explaining each change. Conclude with three specific documentation habits this writer should develop.\n\n[Paste clinical note for review]","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS FRAMEWORKS (13-22) ---
    {"id":"med-13","title":"Differential Diagnosis Teaching Framework","prompt":"You are a clinical reasoning educator at a medical school running a problem-based learning session. Using the fictional case of a 35-year-old woman presenting with four weeks of progressive dyspnea on exertion, palpitations, lower extremity edema, and a new systolic murmur heard best at the left sternal border, teach medical students how to build a structured differential diagnosis. Walk through the following framework: (1) identifying the illness script (acute vs. subacute vs. chronic, anatomical localization, pathophysiology categories), (2) generating a broad initial differential using the VINDICATE mnemonic (Vascular, Infectious, Neoplastic, Degenerative, Idiopathic, Congenital, Autoimmune, Trauma, Endocrine/Metabolic), (3) narrowing the differential using must-not-miss diagnoses vs. most-likely diagnoses, (4) prioritizing the work-up based on clinical probability and consequences of missing, and (5) reassessing the differential as new data arrives. List the top 6 diagnoses for this case with brief reasoning for each. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","medical-education","clinical-reasoning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-14","title":"Chest Pain Differential Diagnosis Educator","prompt":"You are an emergency medicine educator writing a teaching module on the differential diagnosis of chest pain for emergency medicine residents. Write a comprehensive framework that organizes chest pain differentials by: (1) immediately life-threatening causes that must always be considered first (ACS, aortic dissection, pulmonary embolism, tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, esophageal rupture), (2) urgent but not immediately life-threatening causes (unstable angina, pericarditis, myocarditis, pneumonia, pleuritis), and (3) non-urgent causes (GERD, musculoskeletal, anxiety/panic disorder, costochondritis, herpes zoster). For each category, describe the typical history features, physical exam findings, and initial diagnostic approach that helps distinguish it. Use a fictional 52-year-old male smoker with diabetics and hypertension as a running example. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","emergency-medicine","cardiology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-15","title":"Headache Differential Diagnosis Guide","prompt":"You are a neurology educator teaching medical students how to approach headache differentially. Write a structured educational guide covering: (1) the critical 'red flag' features that suggest a dangerous secondary headache requiring urgent work-up (thunderclap onset, worst headache of life, new headache in patient over 50, headache with fever and neck stiffness, progressive headache worsening over weeks, headache with neurological deficits, headache with visual changes or jaw claudication), (2) the major categories of primary headache disorder (migraine with and without aura, tension-type, cluster, medication overuse), (3) important secondary headache causes by mechanism (vascular, infectious, mass lesion, CSF pressure disorders, metabolic), and (4) how to use the history alone to differentiate migraine from tension headache from cluster using specific discriminating features. Use fictional patient vignettes for three different headache presentations. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","neurology","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-16","title":"Abdominal Pain Differential Framework","prompt":"You are a general surgery and internal medicine educator co-teaching a session on the systematic approach to abdominal pain. Write an educational framework that teaches students to organize the differential by: (1) anatomical quadrant and organ-based thinking (what structures lie in each quadrant and what pathology affects each), (2) character of pain (colicky vs. constant, visceral vs. parietal peritoneal), (3) associated symptoms as discriminating features (fever, jaundice, vomiting, diarrhea, blood in stool, dysuria, vaginal discharge), (4) must-not-miss diagnoses regardless of presentation (appendicitis, ectopic pregnancy in reproductive-age women, mesenteric ischemia, ruptured AAA), and (5) age and sex-specific considerations. Write three fictional one-paragraph vignettes illustrating right lower quadrant pain in a 20-year-old woman, epigastric pain in a 55-year-old male, and diffuse abdominal pain in a 70-year-old, with the differential for each. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","surgery","gastroenterology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-17","title":"Fever of Unknown Origin Teaching Case","prompt":"You are an infectious disease educator conducting a teaching rounds session on Fever of Unknown Origin (FUO). Write a comprehensive educational guide using a fictional case: a 48-year-old immunocompetent male with three weeks of daily fevers reaching 39.2°C, 8kg weight loss, night sweats, and fatigue, with initial basic work-up unrevealing. Teach the classic FUO definition and the four categories (classic, nosocomial, immune-deficient, HIV-associated), then walk through the diagnostic approach systematically: detailed history questions that are often overlooked (travel, animal exposures, sick contacts, unusual foods, medications, dental work, sexual history), physical examination pearls (lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, cardiac murmurs, fundoscopic exam, skin findings), tiered laboratory and imaging work-up rationale, and when to consider empiric treatment vs. watchful waiting. List the top causes of FUO in each category. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","infectious-disease","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-18","title":"Altered Mental Status Differential Framework","prompt":"You are a neurologist and intensivist educator writing a teaching guide on the differential diagnosis and initial approach to altered mental status (AMS) for internal medicine interns. Write a systematic framework covering: (1) defining AMS and distinguishing delirium, dementia, and obtundation for documentation and communication purposes, (2) the AEIOU TIPS mnemonic for acute AMS causes (Alcohol/toxins, Epilepsy, Infection, Oxygen/metabolic, Uremia/renal, Trauma, Insulin/glucose, Psychiatric, Stroke/structural), (3) a focused emergency assessment approach, (4) how to distinguish delirium from dementia from psychiatric illness using history and mental status exam features, and (5) reversible vs. irreversible causes. Use a fictional 78-year-old nursing home patient presenting with acute confusion and agitation as a teaching case throughout. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","neurology","emergency-medicine"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-19","title":"Dyspnea Differential Diagnosis Educator","prompt":"You are a pulmonology and cardiology educator writing a workshop guide on dyspnea for third-year medical students. Organize the differential diagnosis of dyspnea into a teaching framework covering: (1) acute vs. chronic onset as an immediate discriminator, (2) major organ system categories (cardiac, pulmonary, hematologic, metabolic, neuromuscular, psychiatric), (3) how specific history features point toward specific diagnoses (orthopnea → heart failure, pleuritic quality → PE or pleuritis, wheezing history → asthma/COPD, exertional-only with recovery → cardiac ischemia or deconditioning), (4) how physical exam findings (JVD, S3 gallop, crackles, wheezing, stridor, peripheral edema, clubbing) narrow the differential, and (5) how to select and sequence initial investigations logically. Write four one-paragraph fictional vignettes representing four different causes of dyspnea with distinct presentations. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","pulmonology","cardiology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-20","title":"Rash Differential Diagnosis Teaching Module","prompt":"You are a dermatology educator writing a teaching module for family medicine residents on the systematic approach to diagnosing skin rashes. Write a guide covering: (1) descriptive terminology students must master before attempting differential diagnosis (macule, papule, plaque, vesicle, bulla, pustule, wheal, purpura, petechiae, telangiectasia, lichenification, excoriation — with definitions and examples), (2) key history questions that differentiate rashes (onset, evolution, distribution, pruritus, systemic symptoms, drug exposures, sick contacts, travel, occupational exposures), (3) a distribution-based framework (face, trunk, extremities, palms and soles, mucous membranes, intertriginous), (4) life-threatening rashes that must never be missed (meningococcemia, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, TEN, necrotizing fasciitis, purpura fulminans), and (5) four common rash presentations with differentials. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","dermatology","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-21","title":"Syncope Differential Diagnosis Guide","prompt":"You are a cardiologist and neurologist co-teaching a session on syncope for internal medicine residents. Write a structured educational guide on the differential diagnosis of syncope covering: (1) the definition of syncope versus near-syncope versus seizure versus drop attacks (with distinguishing features), (2) the three major categories (reflex/vasovagal, orthostatic hypotension, cardiac — arrhythmic and structural), (3) red flag features that suggest cardiac or high-risk etiology (syncope during exertion, no prodrome, palpitations preceding syncope, family history of sudden cardiac death, structural heart disease, syncope while supine, patient age over 60), (4) the role of history, physical exam, ECG, and selected investigations in the initial evaluation, and (5) risk stratification tools used in clinical practice. Use three fictional cases representing vasovagal, orthostatic, and arrhythmic syncope to contrast the presentations. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","cardiology","neurology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-22","title":"Build a Differential Diagnosis from a Clinical Case","prompt":"You are a clinical reasoning educator helping a medical student or resident develop their differential diagnosis skills. Review the following clinical case details and produce: (1) a broad initial differential diagnosis organized by anatomical location and pathophysiological mechanism, (2) a 'must not miss' list of dangerous diagnoses that should always be considered regardless of probability, (3) the most likely diagnoses ranked by clinical probability with brief reasoning for each, (4) the key discriminating historical and physical exam features that would move diagnoses up or down the list, (5) a prioritized diagnostic work-up with rationale for each test, and (6) a teaching commentary on what reasoning errors students commonly make with this type of presentation. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your clinical case details]","tags":["medicine","differential-diagnosis","clinical-reasoning","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:45:00Z","featured":true},

    // --- MEDICAL WRITING & RESEARCH (23-38) ---
    {"id":"med-23","title":"Medical Research Abstract Writer","prompt":"You are a medical writing educator and researcher teaching health professionals how to write structured scientific abstracts. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the purpose and function of an abstract (the most-read part of any paper), the standard structured format (Background/Objective, Methods, Results, Conclusion) and what belongs in each section, the importance of including quantitative results with confidence intervals and p-values where applicable, how to write a results section that is factual without interpretation, how to write conclusions that are proportionate to the evidence presented, common abstract writing errors (overstating findings, burying the main result, omitting methods limitations), and word count discipline. Then write an exemplary structured abstract using a fictional randomized controlled trial on the effect of a dietary intervention on HbA1c levels in type 2 diabetes patients (n=320, 6-month trial). For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","research","academic"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-24","title":"Case Report Writing Guide (CARE Guidelines)","prompt":"You are a medical journal editor and educator writing a guide for clinicians on how to write a publishable medical case report following the CARE (CAse REport) guidelines. Write a section-by-section guide covering: Title (informative, includes diagnosis or key finding), Abstract (structured with timeline and learning points), Introduction (why this case is worth reporting — rare presentation, unusual complication, diagnostic challenge, or treatment innovation), Patient Information (demographics, presenting complaints, medical and social history — anonymized), Clinical Findings (physical exam, key investigations), Timeline (chronological summary), Diagnostic Assessment (working and final diagnoses, reasoning), Therapeutic Interventions, Follow-up and Outcomes, Discussion (literature review, case comparison, reasoning for unusual features, limitations), Patient Perspective (if available), and Informed Consent. After the guide, write a sample case report outline for a fictional case of a 30-year-old presenting with an atypical manifestation of a common condition. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","case-report","academic"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-25","title":"Systematic Review Methodology Guide","prompt":"You are a research methodology educator teaching clinicians how to conduct and write a systematic review and meta-analysis. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the difference between a narrative review, systematic review, and meta-analysis, the PICO framework for formulating a systematic review question (Population, Intervention, Comparator, Outcome), registering a protocol with PROSPERO, developing a search strategy with Boolean operators and MeSH terms, screening and data extraction workflow (PRISMA flow diagram), risk of bias assessment tools (Cochrane ROB2, Newcastle-Ottawa Scale for observational studies), how to handle heterogeneity in meta-analysis (I-squared statistic, fixed vs. random effects models), forest plots and funnel plots explained for a non-statistician, and the GRADE framework for rating quality of evidence. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","systematic-review","research-methodology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-26","title":"Clinical Trial Protocol Writing","prompt":"You are a clinical research educator writing a guide for physician-researchers on the structure of a clinical trial protocol. Write a comprehensive guide covering all major protocol sections: Background and Rationale (literature review, scientific premise), Study Objectives and Endpoints (primary, secondary, and exploratory), Study Design (randomized vs. non-randomized, blinding, parallel vs. crossover, phase designation), Study Population (inclusion and exclusion criteria, sample size justification), Interventions (description, dosing, administration), Randomization and Blinding Procedures, Data Collection and Management, Statistical Analysis Plan, Safety Monitoring (AE/SAE definitions, stopping rules, DSMB), Ethical Considerations (IRB, informed consent, Declaration of Helsinki), and Publication Plan. Then write a sample protocol outline for a fictional Phase 2 trial of a new antihypertensive agent in treatment-resistant hypertension. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","clinical-trials","research"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-27","title":"Medical Manuscript Peer Review Guide","prompt":"You are a senior editor at a medical journal conducting a workshop on peer review for junior faculty and fellows. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the purpose and importance of peer review in medical publishing, how to decide whether to accept a review invitation, how to read a manuscript critically and systematically (first pass for big picture, second pass for detailed critique), the structure of a peer review report (summary, major concerns, minor concerns, specific line comments), evaluating different sections (background, methods, results, discussion, abstract, figures and tables, statistical methods), identifying fatal flaws vs. major vs. minor revisions, writing constructive and actionable feedback that helps authors improve the work, maintaining confidentiality and avoiding conflicts of interest, and the difference between reviewer and editor roles. Include examples of good and poor reviewer comments on the same fictional manuscript section. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","peer-review","academic"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-28","title":"Grant Proposal Writing for Medical Researchers","prompt":"You are a research funding educator teaching faculty and fellows how to write competitive NIH-style grant applications. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the anatomy of an NIH R01 application (Specific Aims page — the most important single page in the application, Significance, Innovation, Approach, Investigators, Environment), how to write a compelling Specific Aims page (opening hook, gap in knowledge, central hypothesis, specific aims, expected outcomes, impact statement), common reasons NIH study sections score proposals low (weak significance, feasibility concerns, lack of innovation, insufficient preliminary data), how to respond to reviewer critiques in a resubmission, budget justification writing, and the role of the NIH Biosketch. Then write a sample Specific Aims page for a fictional study investigating the mechanisms linking sleep apnea to atrial fibrillation risk. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","grants","research-funding"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:25:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-29","title":"Medical Review Article Writing Framework","prompt":"You are a senior clinician-educator teaching trainees and junior faculty how to write a high-quality narrative review article for a clinical medical journal. Write a step-by-step guide covering: how to choose a review topic that is timely and has an audience, how to scope the review appropriately (not too broad, not too narrow), literature search strategy and source selection hierarchy (meta-analyses and systematic reviews first, then RCTs, then observational studies, then expert opinion), structuring the review (introduction, epidemiology and burden of disease, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic approach, treatment — current evidence and controversies, future directions, conclusion), writing techniques that make reviews readable and clinically actionable, using tables and figures to summarize evidence efficiently, and how to handle conflicting evidence honestly. Write a detailed outline for a fictional review on the management of recurrent Clostridioides difficile infection. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","review-article","academic"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-30","title":"Plain Language Medical Writing Guide","prompt":"You are a health literacy educator teaching clinicians how to communicate medical information in plain language accessible to patients and the general public. Write a comprehensive guide covering: why health literacy matters (nearly half of adults read at or below an 8th-grade level, low health literacy is associated with worse outcomes), the core principles of plain language writing (active voice, short sentences, common words instead of medical jargon, second-person 'you' address, logical organization), the Flesch-Kincaid readability test and how to use it, translating specific medical terms into plain language (a table of 20 common medical terms and their plain language equivalents), structuring patient-facing documents (most important information first, use of headers and bullet points, white space), and testing readability with real patients using the Teach-Back method. Then rewrite a fictional complex medical paragraph from a specialist letter in plain language. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","health-literacy","patient-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-31","title":"Pharmaceutical Medical Writing Overview","prompt":"You are a pharmaceutical industry educator writing a guide for physicians and scientists considering a career in medical writing. Write a comprehensive overview covering: the types of documents pharmaceutical medical writers produce (clinical study reports, regulatory submissions including CTD modules, Investigator's Brochures, informed consent forms, clinical overview and summary documents, manuscripts, abstracts, posters, slide decks, product labeling, patient information leaflets), the regulatory framework governing pharmaceutical documents (ICH guidelines, FDA and EMA requirements, GCP), the difference between a medical writer and a scientific writer in industry, core skills needed (attention to detail, regulatory knowledge, ability to synthesize complex data, strict deadline adherence), the AMWA and ISMPP professional organizations, and typical career paths. Include a section on the ethical responsibilities of pharmaceutical medical writers regarding data transparency and accurate representation of results. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","pharmaceutical","career"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-32","title":"Statistics in Medical Research — Plain English Guide","prompt":"You are a biostatistics educator writing a guide for clinicians who want to critically read medical literature but who are not statisticians. Write a plain-language explanation of the statistical concepts that appear most commonly in medical papers: descriptive statistics (mean, median, mode, standard deviation, interquartile range — when to use each), probability and distributions, hypothesis testing and p-values (what p < 0.05 actually means and what it does not mean), confidence intervals and why they are more informative than p-values alone, types of error (Type I and Type II), statistical power and sample size, common statistical tests and when they are used (t-test, chi-square, ANOVA, Mann-Whitney, log-rank), relative risk vs. odds ratio vs. absolute risk reduction vs. number needed to treat, Kaplan-Meier survival curves, and multivariate analysis. Use fictional data examples throughout to illustrate each concept. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","statistics","research-methodology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-33","title":"Medical Poster Design and Writing Guide","prompt":"You are a medical educator and conference presenter teaching residents and students how to create an effective scientific poster for a medical conference. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the purpose and audience of a conference poster, standard dimensions and layout (title, authors, introduction, methods, results, conclusions, acknowledgments, references, contact information), principles of visual design for scientific posters (color scheme, font size hierarchy, white space, figure-to-text ratio), how to write each section concisely (the 30-second rule — viewers should get the main message in 30 seconds), how to create effective tables and figures (each should be independently interpretable), common poster mistakes (too much text, unreadable fonts, missing key data, unclear conclusions), how to prepare for poster presentation (elevator pitch, handling questions), and emerging trends (digital and animated posters). Then write a sample title and abstract for a fictional quality improvement poster on reducing central line infections in an ICU. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","presentation","academic"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-34","title":"Writing a Letter to the Editor","prompt":"You are a medical journal editor teaching clinicians how to write an effective Letter to the Editor (LTE) for a medical journal. Write a step-by-step guide covering: the purpose of LTEs (to comment on published research, raise methodological concerns, report additional data, describe an unusual case rapidly), the standard structure (reference to the original article with citation, specific point being raised with evidence, brief conclusion), word count and citation limits for major journals, how to make a scientific argument concisely, how to raise a methodological criticism constructively without being dismissive of the authors' work, how to disclose conflicts of interest, and the submission and response process. Then write two sample LTEs: (1) a methodological critique of a fictional trial on antibiotic duration for community-acquired pneumonia, and (2) a brief clinical observation adding to findings from a fictional cohort study on heart failure outcomes. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","academic","peer-review"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-35","title":"Review and Improve a Medical Document","prompt":"You are a senior medical writer and editor with expertise in both clinical medicine and academic publishing. Review the following medical document carefully and provide structured editorial feedback covering: (1) clarity and logical flow of argument or narrative, (2) accuracy and completeness of medical terminology and concepts, (3) writing quality (active vs. passive voice, sentence length, redundancy, jargon), (4) structure and organization, (5) appropriateness of tone for the intended audience (clinicians, researchers, patients — identify which this document targets), (6) any factual statements that appear unsupported or require citation, and (7) specific line edits for the five most important improvements. Then provide a revised version of the first 200 words incorporating your suggestions. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your medical document or draft]","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","editing","academic"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-36","title":"Conference Abstract Submission Guide","prompt":"You are a medical educator and experienced conference presenter writing a guide for residents and students preparing their first academic conference abstract submission. Cover: how to choose the right conference for your work (scope, prestige, submission deadlines, oral vs. poster competition), the typical structured abstract format (Background, Objective, Methods, Results, Conclusions) with strict word limits, how to write a compelling title that includes the main finding, how to present results with actual numbers not vague statements, how to write a conclusion that is proportionate to your evidence, how to select appropriate keywords and categories, common reasons abstracts are rejected (insufficient data, overstated conclusions, poor organization, missing statistical support), and how to respond if your abstract is accepted as a poster vs. oral presentation. Write a before-and-after example showing a weak fictional abstract improved to publication-ready standard. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-writing","academic","conference"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-37","title":"Evidence-Based Medicine Critical Appraisal Framework","prompt":"You are an evidence-based medicine educator teaching a workshop on critical appraisal of medical literature. Write a comprehensive guide covering the critical appraisal of three study types: (1) Randomized Controlled Trial — use the CONSORT checklist framework to evaluate validity (randomization method, allocation concealment, blinding, intention-to-treat analysis, follow-up completeness), results (effect size, confidence intervals, NNT), and applicability; (2) Cohort Study — use STROBE checklist framework to evaluate selection bias, information bias, confounding, and temporal relationship; (3) Systematic Review/Meta-analysis — use PRISMA and GRADE frameworks to evaluate search completeness, heterogeneity, publication bias, and certainty of evidence. For each study type, include a structured set of questions to ask and a fictional abstract with teaching commentary pointing out methodological strengths and weaknesses. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","evidence-based-medicine","medical-education","research-methodology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-38","title":"Appraise a Medical Study","prompt":"You are an experienced evidence-based medicine educator and clinical researcher. Critically appraise the following medical research document using a structured framework. Cover: (1) study design identification and appropriateness for the research question, (2) population (inclusion/exclusion criteria, representativeness, sample size and power), (3) intervention/exposure and comparison group, (4) outcome measures (primary and secondary, validity and clinical relevance), (5) risk of bias (specific to study type — randomization, allocation concealment, blinding, selection bias, confounding, measurement bias), (6) statistical methods and results interpretation (effect sizes, confidence intervals, p-values, clinical vs. statistical significance), (7) generalizability to real clinical practice, and (8) overall strength of evidence on the GRADE scale. Provide a summary verdict: 'Ready to change practice / Promising but needs confirmation / Insufficient evidence.' For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste the study abstract or key sections]","tags":["medicine","evidence-based-medicine","critical-appraisal","research-methodology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:15:00Z","featured":true},

    // --- MEDICAL EDUCATION & TRAINING (39-52) ---
    {"id":"med-39","title":"Oral Case Presentation Teaching Guide","prompt":"You are a clinical educator running a workshop on oral case presentations for third-year medical students entering their clerkship rotations. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the purpose of the oral presentation in clinical medicine (communication tool, demonstrates clinical reasoning, invites teaching), the difference in format between a full new patient presentation and a brief work-round presentation, the standard sequence for a full presentation (one-liner, HPI, PMH/PSH/medications/allergies/social/family/ROS, physical exam findings, data, assessment and plan), how to deliver a one-liner that captures the essence of the case in 15-20 words, how to present the assessment and plan by active problem with your own differential and reasoning, how to handle questions from the attending, common presentation mistakes (reading from notes, leading with the diagnosis before presenting the story, reporting every single lab value), and how to present a challenging case when you are uncertain. Include a fictional sample one-liner and assessment section. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-education","clinical-skills","residency-training"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-40","title":"Medical School Curriculum Design Framework","prompt":"You are a medical education specialist and curriculum designer writing a guide for faculty developing or reforming a medical school curriculum. Write a comprehensive framework covering: the major approaches to medical education (organ-based integrated curriculum, problem-based learning, case-based learning, team-based learning, flipped classroom, simulation), applying Bloom's taxonomy to medical education objectives (knowledge, comprehension, application, analysis, synthesis, evaluation), the role of standardized patients and clinical simulation, integrating basic and clinical sciences vertically across the curriculum, developing meaningful assessment (formative vs. summative, OSCEs, written examinations, portfolio assessment, workplace-based assessment), teaching clinical reasoning explicitly, addressing professionalism and medical ethics across the curriculum, and the role of feedback and remediation. Include a sample one-semester module outline for a second-year medical student cardiovascular block. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-education","curriculum-design","teaching"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-41","title":"Simulation Scenario Design for Medical Training","prompt":"You are a clinical simulation educator and director of a medical simulation center writing a guide for faculty on designing high-quality simulation scenarios for medical training. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the educational objectives that simulation is best suited for (procedural skills, teamwork and communication, crisis resource management, rare events), the components of a simulation scenario (learner level and background, learning objectives, patient information and setting, confederate roles, moulage and environmental setup, scenario flow and branch points, critical actions checklist, debriefing guide), principles of good debriefing ('advocacy-inquiry' technique, psychological safety, video review), fidelity considerations (high vs. low vs. hybrid fidelity), integrating simulation into a larger curriculum, and measuring simulation effectiveness. Write a complete simulation scenario template for a fictional cardiac arrest in the emergency department training scenario for emergency medicine residents. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-education","simulation","clinical-skills"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-42","title":"Teaching Bedside Physical Examination Skills","prompt":"You are a clinical examination skills educator running a small-group teaching session for second-year medical students beginning their clinical skills training. Write a comprehensive guide for teaching the cardiovascular physical examination at the bedside. Cover: creating a respectful and professional examination environment, proper positioning and draping, systematic examination sequence (inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation), what to look for on inspection (general appearance, respiratory distress, cyanosis, clubbing, edema, jugular venous pressure estimation), palpation of the precordium (apex beat location and character, heaves and thrills), auscultation technique (diaphragm vs. bell, positioning, cardiac cycle timing, radiation of murmurs), describing heart sounds and murmurs using standardized language, and integrating findings to form a clinical impression. Include teaching tips for common student errors in each step. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-education","clinical-skills","physical-examination"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-43","title":"Resident Feedback and Evaluation Framework","prompt":"You are a graduate medical education director writing a guide for attending physicians on how to deliver effective feedback and complete meaningful evaluations for residents. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the difference between feedback and evaluation, why effective feedback is the cornerstone of competency-based medical education, the principles of effective feedback (timely, specific, based on direct observation, behaviorally anchored, actionable, forward-looking), common feedback delivery models (Pendleton's rules, the one-minute preceptor, SHARP, R2C2), how to give feedback on sensitive performance issues (professionalism, communication, clinical judgment), how to write a meaningful competency-based evaluation vs. a generic glowing narrative, how to identify and manage a struggling resident early, and the resident's role in seeking and receiving feedback. Include a before-and-after example of a vague vs. specific fictional resident evaluation narrative. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-education","residency-training","faculty-development"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-44","title":"Medical Ethics Teaching Case Studies","prompt":"You are a medical ethics educator designing a series of teaching cases for medical students and residents. Write four detailed fictional case vignettes covering core ethical principles and dilemmas: (1) Autonomy and informed consent — a competent adult patient refusing a life-saving blood transfusion on religious grounds, (2) Beneficence vs. non-maleficence — a family requesting 'everything possible' for a terminally ill patient where aggressive intervention would cause suffering without benefit, (3) Justice and resource allocation — triaging two patients for the last ICU bed, (4) Confidentiality and duty to warn — a patient discloses a serious communicable disease to a physician and refuses to inform their partner. For each case, present the ethical dilemma clearly, identify the competing principles, present the range of defensible positions, describe what process should be followed, and suggest discussion questions for the teaching group. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-ethics","medical-education","clinical-skills"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:55:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-45","title":"How to Write Effective Multiple Choice Questions for Medical Exams","prompt":"You are a medical education assessment specialist writing a guide for faculty on how to write high-quality multiple choice questions (MCQs) for medical licensing-style examinations. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the components of a well-constructed clinical vignette MCQ (patient demographics, presenting complaint and history, physical exam, investigations, lead-in question, answer options), taxonomy of MCQ cognitive levels (recall, comprehension, application, clinical decision-making — with examples of each), the 'one best answer' format vs. 'extended matching' questions, characteristics of good distractors (plausible but clearly distinguishable for a competent student), technical flaws to avoid (absolute qualifiers like 'always/never', grammatical clues, overlapping options, 'all of the above / none of the above', trick questions, unfair specificity), and the item review and validation process. Write five sample MCQs at different cognitive levels using fictional clinical vignettes covering cardiology, infectious disease, and pediatrics. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-education","assessment","examination"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-46","title":"Clinical Reasoning Thinking Aloud Exercise","prompt":"You are a clinical reasoning educator using 'think-aloud' methodology to teach diagnostic reasoning to medical students. Read the following patient presentation and model expert clinical reasoning by thinking aloud through each step: (1) what are the most prominent features of this presentation and what is my immediate mental model, (2) what hypotheses do I activate early and why, (3) what additional information am I seeking and what would each answer mean, (4) what are the cognitive biases I need to guard against in this case (availability heuristic, anchoring, premature closure, framing effect), (5) how does new information shift my probability estimates, and (6) what is my final diagnostic synthesis and plan reasoning. After the think-aloud, write a teaching commentary identifying the key reasoning strategies used and lessons for learners. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your clinical case presentation]","tags":["medicine","medical-education","clinical-reasoning","diagnostic-excellence"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-47","title":"Motivational Interviewing in Clinical Practice","prompt":"You are a behavioral health educator teaching primary care physicians and nurses motivational interviewing (MI) techniques for use in clinical practice. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the evidence base for MI in health behavior change, the four processes of MI (engaging, focusing, evoking, planning), the OARS communication skills (Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, Summarizing), recognizing and responding to change talk and sustain talk, rolling with resistance (avoiding the confrontation-denial trap), the spirit of MI (partnership, acceptance, compassion, evocation), applying MI to common clinical scenarios (smoking cessation, weight loss counseling, medication adherence, alcohol use reduction), the difference between an MI-consistent and MI-inconsistent response, and how to practice MI skills under time pressure in a brief clinical encounter. Include a sample 5-minute MI dialogue between a provider and a patient considering quitting smoking. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","behavioral-health","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-48","title":"Prepare a Teaching Case Presentation","prompt":"You are a senior clinician helping a resident or student prepare an outstanding teaching case presentation for morning report or grand rounds. Using the clinical details provided, help structure and polish the presentation by: (1) crafting a compelling one-liner that captures the clinical essence, (2) organizing the history in a logical sequence that builds clinical suspense without obscuring important diagnostic clues, (3) identifying the two or three key pivotal findings that drive the differential, (4) structuring a well-reasoned differential diagnosis with supporting and refuting features for each diagnosis, (5) proposing a logical and cost-effective work-up strategy, (6) identifying three or four teaching points appropriate for the audience, and (7) suggesting one focused literature reference or guideline to anchor the teaching. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your clinical case details]","tags":["medicine","medical-education","case-presentation","residency-training"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-49","title":"Pathophysiology Teaching Explainer","prompt":"You are a pathophysiology educator at a medical school known for making complex mechanisms clear and memorable. Write a detailed, teaching-quality explanation of the pathophysiology of acute decompensated heart failure, starting from the molecular and cellular level and building upward to the clinical manifestations. Cover: normal cardiac physiology (Starling's law, preload, afterload, contractility), the initial compensatory mechanisms that are activated when cardiac output falls (RAAS activation, sympathetic nervous system activation, cardiac remodeling), how these compensatory mechanisms become maladaptive and contribute to progression, the mechanisms responsible for pulmonary edema (hydrostatic pressure vs. oncotic pressure), the mechanisms underlying peripheral edema and ascites, and how the pathophysiology explains each clinical feature (dyspnea, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, S3 gallop, JVD, edema, fatigue, cool extremities). Use analogies and diagrams described in text to make the mechanisms visual and memorable. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pathophysiology","medical-education","cardiology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-50","title":"Anatomy Teaching Case Integration","prompt":"You are an anatomy educator integrating clinical case teaching into the gross anatomy curriculum for first-year medical students. Write four short clinical case vignettes specifically designed to teach anatomical concepts through clinical application: (1) a patient with wrist drop after a humeral shaft fracture — teaches the course and vulnerability of the radial nerve, (2) a patient with ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on one side of the face — teaches the sympathetic chain and Horner syndrome anatomy, (3) a patient with appendicitis presenting initially as periumbilical pain that migrates to the right lower quadrant — teaches referred pain, visceral vs. parietal peritoneum innervation, and McBurney's point, (4) a patient with a femoral hernia presenting as a groin mass below the inguinal ligament — teaches the femoral triangle, femoral canal, and the boundaries that distinguish femoral from inguinal hernia. For each case, include the anatomy teaching points and a labeled diagram description. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","anatomy","medical-education","clinical-correlation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-51","title":"Pharmacology Teaching by Drug Class","prompt":"You are a clinical pharmacology educator writing a teaching guide for second-year medical students on the beta-adrenergic antagonist (beta-blocker) drug class. Write a comprehensive, clinically oriented pharmacology guide covering: mechanism of action (beta-1 vs. beta-2 receptor selectivity, intrinsic sympathomimetic activity, membrane-stabilizing activity), pharmacokinetics (lipophilicity and CNS penetration, hepatic vs. renal elimination, key differences among agents), major clinical indications with evidence base (heart failure, post-MI, hypertension, atrial fibrillation rate control, migraine prophylaxis, essential tremor, hyperthyroidism, portal hypertension), contraindications and precautions (decompensated heart failure, reactive airway disease, bradycardia, heart block, cocaine toxicity — explain the mechanism of each), drug interactions, major adverse effects with mechanisms, and how to choose among agents for specific clinical scenarios. End with five clinical vignette-based practice questions. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pharmacology","medical-education","clinical-skills"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-52","title":"Write a Teaching Script for a Clinical Skill","prompt":"You are a clinical skills educator who has been asked to develop a structured teaching script for a procedure or clinical skill workshop. Using the details provided, write a complete teaching script that includes: (1) a brief introduction explaining the clinical relevance and learning objectives, (2) a step-by-step procedural walkthrough in the order it would be performed, with each step narrated in clear instructional language, (3) 'teaching pause' annotations marking where the instructor should check learner understanding or demonstrate a technique deliberately slowly, (4) common errors to anticipate and how to identify and correct them in real time, (5) safety considerations and how to handle an adverse event during training, (6) questions to ask learners to check conceptual understanding, and (7) a brief assessment criteria checklist for sign-off. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste the name and brief description of the clinical skill or procedure to teach]","tags":["medicine","medical-education","clinical-skills","simulation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:35:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- HEALTHCARE COMMUNICATION (53-64) ---
    {"id":"med-53","title":"Breaking Bad News — SPIKES Protocol Teaching Guide","prompt":"You are a communication skills educator teaching residents and medical students how to deliver serious news to patients and families. Write a comprehensive guide to the SPIKES protocol (Setting, Perception, Invitation, Knowledge, Empathy, Strategy and Summary) for breaking bad news. For each step, explain the rationale, provide specific language examples (both good and poor phrasing), and describe common errors. Then write a complete fictional scenario transcript using SPIKES: a 52-year-old man is being told by his oncologist that his biopsy has confirmed pancreatic cancer. Include the physician's spoken words, the patient's realistic reactions, how the physician responds to emotional responses, how prognosis is discussed honestly but compassionately, and how the conversation is closed with a clear next step. After the transcript, write a debriefing guide with five discussion questions for teaching groups. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","medical-education","oncology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:50:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-54","title":"Difficult Conversations in Medicine — Teaching Framework","prompt":"You are a physician communication educator writing a guide for residents on navigating five types of difficult conversations encountered in clinical practice. Write a structured teaching guide for each: (1) Disclosing a medical error to a patient — covering the ethical and legal framework, what to say (and what not to say), how to respond to anger, and documentation; (2) Discussing Do-Not-Resuscitate status with a patient and family — when to raise it, how to frame it, and the difference between a DNR and withdrawal of care; (3) Delivering a terminal prognosis to a patient who asks directly 'How long do I have?'; (4) Confronting a colleague about a patient safety concern — using structured communication tools like CUS and SBAR; (5) Setting limits with a patient who is demanding inappropriate care or controlled substances. For each scenario, include a brief model dialogue and a teaching commentary. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","medical-education","ethics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-55","title":"Patient-Centered Communication Skills","prompt":"You are a patient communication educator writing a guide for medical students and residents on the principles of patient-centered communication. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the evidence that patient-centered communication improves outcomes and patient satisfaction, the difference between disease-centered and patient-centered communication, eliciting the patient's perspective (the FIFE framework — Feelings, Ideas about cause, Function impact, Expectations from the visit), active listening techniques and avoiding premature closure of the patient's narrative, responding to emotion (the NURSE acronym — Name, Understand, Respect, Support, Explore), explaining diagnoses and treatment plans clearly (avoiding jargon, checking understanding with teach-back), shared decision-making as a framework for treatment discussions, and special communication challenges (cross-cultural communication, language barriers, low health literacy, discussing sensitive topics). Include five annotated dialogue examples illustrating good and poor communication. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","medical-education","patient-centered-care"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-56","title":"SBAR Communication Tool for Healthcare Teams","prompt":"You are a patient safety and communication educator writing a guide on the SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) communication framework for nurses, physicians, and other healthcare professionals. Write a comprehensive guide covering: why structured communication reduces medical errors (Joint Commission data on communication failures in sentinel events), the four components of SBAR with detailed explanation of what belongs in each section, how SBAR differs in urgency (routine handoff vs. urgent clinical deterioration), adapting SBAR for different clinical scenarios (nurse to physician call, physician to consultant, handoff between shifts, emergency escalation), how SBAR improves shared mental models in teams, how to use SBAR in written communication (text page, electronic message), and common SBAR errors (vague situation statements, omitting the recommendation, burying the urgency). Write four complete sample SBAR communications for fictional clinical scenarios at different urgency levels. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","patient-safety","teamwork"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-57","title":"Telemedicine Consultation Skills Guide","prompt":"You are a telemedicine educator writing a comprehensive guide for clinicians transitioning to or expanding their practice to include telehealth visits. Write a guide covering: the evidence base for telemedicine effectiveness across specialties, technical prerequisites and platform setup for optimal visit quality (camera positioning, lighting, background, audio), how to open a telemedicine visit and establish rapport through a screen, the telehealth-adapted history taking (more emphasis on history when physical exam is limited), what aspects of the physical examination can be guided remotely (visual inspection, patient self-examination with guidance, peripheral pulse, skin assessment), how to document a telemedicine visit appropriately (noting technical quality, what exam was and was not possible), prescribing considerations in telemedicine, when to require in-person follow-up, and patient preparation instructions for a high-quality telemedicine visit. Include a structured telemedicine visit checklist. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","telemedicine","communication","clinical-documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-58","title":"Communicating with Patients about Medications","prompt":"You are a clinical pharmacist and patient education specialist writing a guide for healthcare providers on how to communicate effectively with patients about their medications. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the scale of medication non-adherence (approximately 50% of patients do not take medications as prescribed) and its consequences, the five key questions every patient should be able to answer about each medication (what it is for, how to take it, what to expect, what side effects to watch for, what to do if a dose is missed), how to use teach-back to confirm understanding, counseling patients on common medication side effects without creating nocebo effects, discussing generic vs. brand name medications, addressing cost barriers to adherence, how to counsel patients who want to stop a medication, counseling about drug-drug and drug-food interactions in plain language, and how to create a simple written medication schedule for a patient on multiple drugs. Include a sample medication counseling dialogue for a new statin prescription. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","pharmacy","patient-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-59","title":"Interpreting for Non-English Speaking Patients","prompt":"You are a health communication and medical interpreter educator writing a guide for clinicians on working effectively with patients who have limited English proficiency. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the legal and ethical obligation to provide language access services in healthcare (Title VI, ACA requirements), the significant risks of using ad hoc interpreters (family members, bilingual staff) vs. trained professional medical interpreters, how to work effectively with an in-person interpreter (positioning, speaking directly to the patient, pace and chunk length, avoiding idioms), how to use telephone and video interpreter services optimally, special challenges (sensitive topics, mental health assessment, end-of-life discussions), how to document the use of an interpreter in the medical record, the difference between language interpretation and cultural mediation, and how to advocate for institutional improvements in language access. Include a structured checklist for the pre-encounter, during, and post-encounter phases of an interpreted medical visit. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","health-equity","patient-safety"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-60","title":"Discharge Counseling Communication Guide","prompt":"You are a clinical nurse educator writing a guide on effective patient discharge counseling. Write a comprehensive guide covering: why the discharge transition is a high-risk period for adverse events (medication errors, readmissions, missed follow-up), the components of a safe discharge teaching session (diagnosis explanation in plain language, medication changes and rationale, activity and diet restrictions, wound care or procedure instructions, follow-up appointments, red flag symptoms requiring return to care), how to assess patient health literacy before tailoring your teaching approach, using teach-back to verify understanding (not just 'do you understand?'), written materials to supplement verbal counseling, engaging a caregiver or family member appropriately, high-risk groups requiring enhanced discharge planning (elderly, patients with low health literacy, patients with complex medication regimens, patients with no primary care provider), and how to document discharge education in the medical record. Write a sample teach-back interaction for a patient being discharged after a myocardial infarction. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","patient-education","patient-safety"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-61","title":"Communication Skills for Delivering Prognosis","prompt":"You are a palliative care communication educator teaching a workshop on discussing prognosis with patients who have serious illness. Write a comprehensive guide covering: why prognostic communication matters (enables informed decisions, supports advance care planning, aligns care with patient values), common barriers physicians face when discussing prognosis (uncertainty, fear of taking away hope, professional discomfort, time pressure), how to frame prognostic discussions (ask-tell-ask approach, aligning with what the patient wants to know), communicating uncertainty honestly without abandoning the patient, the 'best case / worst case / most likely case' framework for discussing prognosis, supporting hope while being truthful (hope for comfort, meaningful time, not necessarily cure), how to respond when a patient or family reacts with denial or anger, and how to document a prognostic conversation. Include a sample dialogue for discussing prognosis with a patient with advanced cancer who asks 'Am I going to die from this?' For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","palliative-care","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-62","title":"Review and Improve a Patient Communication Document","prompt":"You are a health literacy specialist and clinical communication expert. Review the following patient-facing document and provide structured feedback covering: (1) overall readability assessment (estimate Flesch-Kincaid reading grade level and note whether it is appropriate for a general patient population), (2) medical jargon that should be replaced with plain language (list specific terms with suggested replacements), (3) sentence length and structure issues, (4) logical organization and flow (does the most important information come first?), (5) formatting improvements (use of headers, bullet points, white space, font recommendations), (6) tone assessment (Is it patronizing? Overly clinical? Anxiety-inducing?), (7) completeness (Is there critical information missing?), and (8) cultural and linguistic considerations. Then provide a fully revised version of the document implementing all your recommendations. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your patient communication document or draft]","tags":["medicine","communication","health-literacy","patient-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-63","title":"Telephone Triage Communication Guide for Nurses","prompt":"You are a clinical nurse educator writing a comprehensive guide on telephone triage communication for nurses working in primary care and hospital triage roles. Write a guide covering: the unique challenges of telephone triage (no visual information, must rely entirely on verbal communication and guided self-report), the legal and liability context of telephone triage (documenting the call accurately, following established protocols, knowing when to escalate), how to open a triage call professionally and establish rapport quickly, systematic information gathering (using open-ended then focused questions, assessing severity without over-alarming the patient, asking about red flag symptoms), how to give clear disposition advice (ED now, urgent same-day appointment, routine appointment, home care advice), how to communicate home care instructions clearly, documentation requirements for telephone triage, and how to manage a caller who is angry or panicking. Include a sample structured triage call for a fictional parent calling about a 2-year-old with fever. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","nursing","communication","triage"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-64","title":"Social Media and Digital Communication for Healthcare Professionals","prompt":"You are a medical education and professional conduct specialist writing a guide for medical students, residents, and physicians on responsible use of social media and digital communication. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the opportunities of social media in medicine (health education, professional networking, advocacy, recruiting for research), the professional and legal risks (patient privacy and HIPAA violations, professional boundary issues, reputation risk, misinformation), specific platform considerations (Twitter/X, LinkedIn, Instagram, TikTok, YouTube — how purpose and audience differ), the AMA guidelines on physician use of social media, what constitutes a HIPAA violation in social media posts (including de-identification pitfalls), how to handle requests to connect with patients on social media, managing online reviews and patient feedback professionally, and creating a professional online presence intentionally. Include a case-based section with five fictional social media scenarios and the correct professional response. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","communication","professionalism","digital-health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:45:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- HEALTHCARE MANAGEMENT & POLICY (65-76) ---
    {"id":"med-65","title":"Quality Improvement in Healthcare — QI Project Guide","prompt":"You are a quality improvement educator writing a guide for residents and clinical staff on how to design and conduct a healthcare quality improvement project. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the difference between QI, research, and audit, the Model for Improvement and PDSA cycle (Plan-Do-Study-Act), defining a clear QI aim statement (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound), the role of process measures, outcome measures, and balancing measures, using the fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram to map causes of a quality problem, run charts and control charts for tracking improvement over time, driver diagrams for planning interventions, common QI methodologies (Lean, Six Sigma, Kaizen), how to engage frontline staff and overcome change resistance, and how to write up a QI project for publication in a peer-reviewed journal. Write a complete QI project framework for a fictional project reducing 30-day readmissions in a heart failure clinic. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","quality-improvement","healthcare-management","patient-safety"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-66","title":"Healthcare Policy Analysis Framework","prompt":"You are a health policy educator writing a guide for clinicians who want to understand and engage with healthcare policy. Write a comprehensive guide covering: why clinicians should be involved in health policy (they have unique expertise and moral authority), the levels of health policy (local institutional, regional, national, international), frameworks for policy analysis (problem identification, stakeholders, options, trade-offs, implementation, evaluation), understanding health economics concepts relevant to policy (cost-effectiveness, QALY, budget impact), major policy levers available to reform healthcare systems (payment reform, regulatory approaches, public health interventions, technology assessment, workforce policy), how to read and critically evaluate a health policy document, how to write a policy brief — a document that translates evidence into actionable recommendations for decision-makers, and how to engage with policymakers effectively (written submissions, legislative testimony, working with advocacy organizations). Write a sample one-page policy brief outline on a fictional policy question regarding telemedicine reimbursement. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","healthcare-policy","health-systems","advocacy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-67","title":"Healthcare Leadership and Management Primer for Physicians","prompt":"You are a physician leadership educator writing a guide for clinicians transitioning into leadership and management roles (department chief, medical director, CMO). Write a comprehensive primer covering: the difference between clinical expertise and leadership skill (and why excellent clinicians are not automatically excellent leaders), core leadership competencies needed in healthcare (vision and strategy, emotional intelligence, team building, change management, conflict resolution, financial literacy), understanding hospital organizational structure and governance, the physician's role in quality, safety, and accreditation, budgeting basics for clinical leaders (understanding revenue cycles, FTE management, capital requests), how to run an effective committee or department meeting, managing underperforming staff and colleagues, building a positive unit culture, and the unique stressors and protective factors in physician leadership roles. Include a self-assessment framework for identifying leadership development priorities. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","healthcare-management","leadership","physician-development"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-68","title":"Patient Safety Culture and Error Reporting","prompt":"You are a patient safety educator writing a guide for healthcare professionals on building a culture of safety and effective error reporting. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the epidemiology of preventable harm in healthcare (To Err is Human landmark report and subsequent data), the difference between a culture of blame and a culture of safety (and how each affects error reporting and learning), just culture principles (distinguishing human error from at-risk behavior from reckless behavior and responding proportionately), how incident reporting systems work and why under-reporting is the norm, how to write an effective incident report, the structure and purpose of Root Cause Analysis (RCA) and Mortality and Morbidity (M&M) conferences, how to respond when you are involved in a medical error (immediate patient disclosure, support for the 'second victim' — the clinician involved), and institutional structures that promote safety (safety huddles, safety coaches, rapid response systems). For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","patient-safety","healthcare-management","quality-improvement"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-69","title":"Healthcare Economics and Value-Based Care","prompt":"You are a health economics educator writing a guide for clinicians on understanding healthcare economics and the shift to value-based care. Write a comprehensive guide covering: basic healthcare economics concepts (supply and demand in healthcare, asymmetric information, moral hazard, adverse selection), how fee-for-service payment creates incentives misaligned with quality and efficiency, the value equation in healthcare (value = quality / cost), value-based payment models (pay-for-performance, bundled payments, accountable care organizations, capitation), the Triple Aim framework (improving population health, enhancing patient experience, reducing per-capita cost), healthcare cost drivers (administrative overhead, pharmaceutical pricing, overtreatment, chronic disease burden), high-value clinical practice (choosing wisely, avoiding low-value care, cost-effectiveness literacy), and how clinicians can contribute to reducing waste without compromising care. Include a table comparing major payment model incentives and their effects on clinical behavior. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","healthcare-economics","health-policy","value-based-care"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-70","title":"Health Equity and Social Determinants of Medicine","prompt":"You are a social medicine and health equity educator writing a guide for medical students and residents on health equity and social determinants of health. Write a comprehensive guide covering: defining health equity vs. health equality vs. health disparities, the social determinants of health framework (economic stability, education, social and community context, health and healthcare access, neighborhood and built environment), how social determinants drive the majority of health outcomes, documented racial and ethnic health disparities in specific conditions (cardiovascular disease, maternal mortality, diabetes, cancer screening) and their structural causes, implicit bias in healthcare and its impact on clinical decision-making (with research evidence), structural racism vs. individual prejudice as a distinction, how clinicians can conduct a social history systematically (PRAPARE tool, housing, food, transportation, immigration status, social isolation), community resources and social prescribing, and how to be an advocate for patients facing social barriers. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","health-equity","social-determinants","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-71","title":"Hospital Infection Control and Prevention Guide","prompt":"You are an infection control educator writing a training guide for clinical staff on healthcare-associated infection (HAI) prevention. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the epidemiology and cost of HAIs (CLABSIs, CAUTIs, SSIs, VAPs, C. difficile, MRSA, VRE), the hierarchy of transmission precautions (standard precautions for all patients, then transmission-based — contact, droplet, airborne), hand hygiene as the single most important intervention (WHO 5 Moments, proper technique, barriers to compliance), proper PPE selection, donning, and doffing sequences, device-associated infection prevention bundles (central line bundle, Foley catheter bundle, ventilator bundle), environmental cleaning and disinfection principles, antimicrobial stewardship as infection prevention, isolation practices and patient communication about isolation, and the role of infection control surveillance. Include a practical quick-reference card format at the end summarizing key precaution types and required PPE. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","infection-control","patient-safety","hospital-medicine"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-72","title":"Healthcare Regulatory and Accreditation Framework","prompt":"You are a healthcare compliance educator writing a guide for clinical leaders and physicians on understanding healthcare regulation and accreditation. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the major regulatory bodies in healthcare (CMS, FDA, state health departments, OSHA) and what they regulate, the Joint Commission and other accreditation bodies (DNV, HFAP) — how accreditation differs from licensure and why hospitals pursue it, the CMS Conditions of Participation and what surveyors look for, how hospital accreditation surveys work (standard surveys, unannounced visits, for-cause surveys), what physicians need to know about medical staff credentialing and privileging, the role of quality metrics in regulatory compliance (core measures, HCAHPS, readmission rates, hospital-acquired conditions), HIPAA basics for clinicians (what constitutes PHI, the minimum necessary standard, breach notification), and how to prepare a department for a regulatory survey. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","healthcare-management","compliance","regulation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-73","title":"Medical Billing and Coding Education for Clinicians","prompt":"You are a clinical documentation and coding educator writing a guide for physicians and advanced practice providers on understanding medical billing and coding. Write a comprehensive guide covering: why documentation-driven coding matters (clinical, operational, and compliance reasons), the structure of ICD-10 coding (diagnosis codes — code to the highest specificity, coding guidelines for common documentation scenarios), CPT coding principles for office visits (E/M coding based on medical decision-making or time — the 2021 AMA revisions), the key elements of medical decision-making documentation (number and complexity of problems, amount and complexity of data, risk of complications), common documentation errors that lead to upcoding or downcoding, the difference between facility and professional coding, modifier use, prior authorization basics, and compliance risks (audit triggers, OIG work plan, False Claims Act implications). Include five before-and-after documentation examples showing how improved clinical documentation translates to more accurate coding. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","billing-coding","clinical-documentation","healthcare-management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-74","title":"Interdisciplinary Team Communication in Healthcare","prompt":"You are a healthcare teamwork educator writing a guide on interdisciplinary team communication for clinical professionals. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the evidence that team-based care improves patient outcomes, the composition and roles of the modern healthcare team (physicians, nurses, pharmacists, social workers, case managers, therapists, chaplains), the principles of effective team communication (shared mental model, closed-loop communication, mutual monitoring, mutual respect across roles), structured communication tools (SBAR, TeamSTEPPS, CUS — I'm Concerned, I'm Uncomfortable, This is a Safety issue), the role of daily interdisciplinary rounds — structure, time management, and who should present, how to navigate hierarchy and power dynamics in healthcare teams, psychological safety and speaking up culture, how to conduct an effective team huddle (safety check-in, anticipated challenges, resource needs), conflict resolution in teams, and TeamSTEPPS principles. Include a fictional annotated interdisciplinary rounds scenario showing best-practice communication. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","teamwork","communication","patient-safety"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-75","title":"Physician Wellbeing and Burnout Prevention","prompt":"You are a physician wellbeing educator and organizational medicine specialist writing a guide on burnout prevention and wellbeing promotion for physicians and residents. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the epidemiology of physician burnout (prevalence rates by specialty, the COVID-19 pandemic impact, recent trends), the Maslach burnout model (emotional exhaustion, depersonalization, reduced personal accomplishment), the difference between individual and systems-level drivers of burnout, the relationship between burnout and patient safety outcomes, evidence-based individual strategies (mindfulness, cognitive reframing, meaning-based coping, schedule boundaries, peer support), institutional interventions with stronger evidence (workload reduction, EHR optimization, scheduling autonomy, debriefing programs, confidential mental health access), the second victim phenomenon and how institutions should respond, recognizing burnout in colleagues and how to initiate a supportive conversation, and resources available for physicians in distress. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","physician-wellbeing","burnout","healthcare-management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-76","title":"Develop a Clinical Guideline or Protocol","prompt":"You are a clinical quality expert and evidence-based medicine specialist. Using the clinical context provided, help develop a clear, evidence-based clinical guideline or standard operating protocol. Structure the document as follows: (1) Title, scope, and target clinical population, (2) Purpose and rationale (clinical problem being addressed, evidence supporting standardization), (3) Definitions of key terms, (4) Step-by-step protocol with decision nodes clearly marked, (5) Specific criteria for escalation or exception from the protocol, (6) Required documentation at each step, (7) Roles and responsibilities of each team member, (8) Monitoring and audit indicators to assess protocol adherence and outcomes, (9) Review and update schedule, (10) References and evidence level for each key recommendation. After the protocol, write a brief implementation guide addressing common barriers to guideline adoption. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your clinical scenario, draft, or key evidence to be incorporated]","tags":["medicine","clinical-guidelines","quality-improvement","healthcare-management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:55:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- CLINICAL GUIDELINES & PHARMACEUTICAL WRITING (77-88) ---
    {"id":"med-77","title":"Clinical Practice Guideline Interpretation Guide","prompt":"You are a clinical educator teaching residents and students how to read, interpret, and apply clinical practice guidelines. Write a comprehensive guide covering: what clinical practice guidelines are and who develops them (professional societies, government bodies, international organizations), the methodology behind guideline development (systematic evidence review, GRADE evidence classification, consensus processes, conflict of interest management), how to read a guideline recommendation — understanding the strength of recommendation (strong vs. conditional/weak) and quality of evidence (high, moderate, low, very low), why strong recommendations based on low-quality evidence exist (and vice versa), how guidelines differ between organizations and what to do when they conflict, how to apply population-level guideline recommendations to individual patients (considering comorbidities, values, and resources), understanding screening recommendations and their trade-offs (sensitivity/specificity, lead-time bias), and how quickly guidelines become outdated. Use a fictional comparison of two conflicting guideline recommendations on the same clinical topic as an example. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","clinical-guidelines","evidence-based-medicine","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-78","title":"Drug Monograph Writing Guide","prompt":"You are a pharmaceutical medical writer and clinical pharmacologist writing a guide on how to write a professional drug monograph for a hospital formulary, educational publication, or clinical reference. Write a comprehensive guide covering the standard sections of a drug monograph: Generic and brand name(s), Drug class and mechanism of action (written clearly for the clinical audience), FDA-approved indications (with distinction from common off-label uses), Pharmacokinetics (absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination — with clinically relevant points highlighted), Dosing in adult and special populations (renal impairment, hepatic impairment, elderly, pediatrics — with dose adjustment rationale), Contraindications and warnings with clinical reasoning, Drug interactions (mechanism and management), Adverse effects organized by frequency and severity, Monitoring parameters, Drug preparation and administration considerations, and Cost and formulary status. Write a complete sample drug monograph for a fictional novel anticoagulant agent using realistic but fictional data. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pharmaceutical-writing","pharmacology","clinical-documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-79","title":"Investigator's Brochure Writing Overview","prompt":"You are a regulatory medical writing educator teaching a workshop for physicians entering the pharmaceutical industry on Investigator's Brochure (IB) writing. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the purpose of the IB (to provide investigators with the scientific and clinical information needed to conduct a clinical trial safely and ethically), the ICH E6 GCP requirements for IBs, the standard IB structure (summary, introduction, physical, chemical, pharmaceutical, nonclinical studies, effects in humans — PK/PD, safety, efficacy, marketed experience, summary of data and guidance), how the IB differs from a product label, how to write the clinical section of an IB drawing on phase 1 and 2 data, how to write the nonclinical summary for a clinical audience, the reference safety information (RSI) section and its role in adverse event reporting during trials, update frequency requirements, and quality review processes. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pharmaceutical-writing","clinical-trials","regulatory"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-80","title":"Patient Information Leaflet Writing Guide","prompt":"You are a regulatory medical writing and health literacy educator writing a guide on how to write a patient information leaflet (PIL) or medication guide that is both regulatory-compliant and genuinely useful to patients. Write a comprehensive guide covering: regulatory requirements for PILs (FDA MedGuide requirements, EMA patient information requirements), the documented problem that most PILs are written at too high a reading level for most patients, applying plain language principles to PIL writing (active voice, direct address, short sentences, common words), the standard PIL structure (what this medicine is and what it is used for, what you need to know before taking it, how to take it, possible side effects, how to store it, contents and further information), risk communication techniques that do not cause unnecessary alarm, presenting side effect frequencies honestly and proportionately, user testing PILs with real patients, and the PIL development and regulatory submission process. Write a sample PIL section for a fictional antihypertensive medication. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pharmaceutical-writing","health-literacy","regulatory"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-81","title":"Pharmacovigilance and Adverse Event Reporting Guide","prompt":"You are a pharmacovigilance educator writing a guide for clinicians on the principles of pharmacovigilance and adverse event reporting. Write a comprehensive guide covering: what pharmacovigilance is and why post-marketing safety surveillance matters (drugs approved on trial data from thousands of patients cannot detect rare events), the regulatory framework (FDA MedWatch, EMA EudraVigilance, WHO VigiBase, national pharmacovigilance centres), how to identify and classify adverse events vs. adverse drug reactions vs. side effects vs. expected pharmacological effects, the four causality categories (definite, probable, possible, unlikely/unrelated), how to complete an adverse event report — what information is needed and common documentation errors, spontaneous reporting rates and under-reporting bias (estimated 90-95% under-reporting), serious adverse event (SAE) definitions and 15-day reporting requirements for clinical trial sites, signal detection in pharmacovigilance, and the role of the REMS program for high-risk medications. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pharmacovigilance","drug-safety","regulatory"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-82","title":"Summarize and Explain Clinical Guidelines","prompt":"You are a clinical educator and evidence-based medicine specialist helping healthcare professionals efficiently understand and apply clinical guidelines. Review the following guideline document or summary and produce: (1) a structured one-page summary of the key recommendations organized by clinical scenario, (2) the strength of evidence grade for each major recommendation, (3) the key changes from the previous guideline version if discernible, (4) three to five clinical situations where the guideline should be applied most directly, (5) three to five common real-world exceptions or nuances where clinician judgment is explicitly needed beyond the guideline, (6) one critical implementation point for clinical practice, and (7) the guideline's own stated limitations or gaps in evidence. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your clinical guideline, recommendation summary, or key sections]","tags":["medicine","clinical-guidelines","evidence-based-medicine","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-83","title":"Antibiotic Stewardship Education Guide","prompt":"You are an infectious disease pharmacist and antimicrobial stewardship educator writing a guide for physicians and nurses on antibiotic stewardship principles. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the antimicrobial resistance crisis — magnitude, mechanisms, and consequences, what antimicrobial stewardship programs (ASPs) are and how they work, core stewardship principles (using antibiotics only when indicated, culture before starting therapy, right drug / right dose / right duration, de-escalation based on culture results, IV-to-oral conversion when appropriate), the most common misuses of antibiotics (treating viral infections, broad-spectrum when narrow-spectrum is sufficient, failing to de-escalate, unnecessarily long courses), how to review cultures and sensitivities to optimize antibiotic selection, how to document clinical decision-making regarding antibiotic use to support stewardship review, and how to communicate antibiotic decisions to patients who expect or demand antibiotics they do not need. Include a decision framework for common community infection scenarios. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","infectious-disease","antimicrobial-stewardship","pharmacy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:40:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-84","title":"Preventive Medicine and Screening Guidelines Overview","prompt":"You are a preventive medicine educator writing a guide for primary care clinicians on evidence-based preventive care and screening. Write a comprehensive educational overview covering: the US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) methodology (how it grades recommendations A through I and D, what each grade means, how evidence is evaluated), the distinction between primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention, key screening recommendations with rationale and evidence for: colorectal cancer (colonoscopy vs. stool-based tests — comparative effectiveness), breast cancer mammography (controversy in 40-49 age group), cervical cancer (Pap smear intervals, HPV co-testing, post-hysterectomy), lung cancer low-dose CT (eligibility criteria), abdominal aortic aneurysm ultrasound, diabetes and prediabetes screening, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and depression. Cover immunization schedules as a prevention tool, chemoprevention where evidence supports it, and counseling interventions with USPSTF evidence (tobacco, alcohol, obesity). For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","preventive-medicine","clinical-guidelines","primary-care"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-85","title":"Palliative Care Principles and Communication Guide","prompt":"You are a palliative care educator writing a comprehensive guide for residents and students on the principles of palliative medicine. Write a guide covering: the definition of palliative care (not synonymous with end-of-life care — it is appropriate at any stage of serious illness), the evidence that early integration of palliative care improves quality of life and sometimes survival, the domains of palliative care (symptom management, psychosocial and spiritual support, communication, goals of care, care coordination), a framework for goals-of-care conversations using the 'REMAP' tool (Reframe, Expect emotion, Map the future, Align with values, Plan), how to assess and document advance care planning (advance directives, healthcare proxy designation, POLST), the distinction between allowing natural death and euthanasia/physician-assisted dying, symptom management principles in palliative care (pain — opioid titration, dyspnea, nausea, delirium), and how to recognize and communicate about actively dying and the final days of life. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","palliative-care","communication","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-86","title":"Medical Ethics Principles and Case Application","prompt":"You are a medical ethics educator teaching a case-based session on the four principles of biomedical ethics (Beauchamp and Childress). Write a comprehensive teaching guide covering: (1) Autonomy — the obligation to respect patients' rights to make informed, voluntary decisions about their own care, including the conditions required for valid autonomous choice (information, understanding, voluntariness, capacity), and when autonomy can be appropriately overridden; (2) Beneficence — the obligation to act in the patient's best interest, including the challenge of defining 'best interest' when values differ; (3) Non-maleficence — the obligation to avoid harm, including the doctrine of double effect and how to reason about treatments that carry significant risks; (4) Justice — the obligation to treat patients fairly and distribute healthcare resources equitably, including macro and micro allocation dilemmas. For each principle, provide a fictional clinical scenario that creates genuine ethical tension, a structured analysis of the competing considerations, and the reasoning process for reaching a defensible position. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-ethics","medical-education","clinical-reasoning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-87","title":"Radiology Ordering Guide — When to Image and Why","prompt":"You are a radiology educator and clinical decision-making specialist writing a guide for medical students and residents on the appropriate use of diagnostic imaging. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the principles of evidence-based imaging (imaging should change management, the question of whether imaging will add information beyond what clinical assessment provides), understanding radiation exposure (doses from common studies, risk communication to patients and clinical decision-making), contrast agent safety (indications for contrast, nephrotoxicity risk stratification, contrast allergy pre-medication), choosing between imaging modalities for common clinical questions (chest pain — when ECG, troponin, CXR, CT angiography, stress test; suspected PE — Wells score, D-dimer, CTPA; head trauma — PECARN and Canadian CT Head rules; abdominal pain — ultrasound first vs. CT), imaging in pregnancy (radiation risk, MRI safety), and the role of imaging utilization guidelines (ACR Appropriateness Criteria). Include a clinical decision table for six common imaging scenarios. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","radiology","clinical-guidelines","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-88","title":"Nutrition in Medicine — Clinical Application Guide","prompt":"You are a clinical nutrition educator writing a guide for medical students and residents on the role of nutrition in clinical medicine, a topic often underemphasized in training. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the importance of nutritional assessment in hospitalized patients (malnutrition prevalence, MUST or NRS-2002 screening tools, consequences of malnutrition on healing, immunity, and outcomes), macronutrient requirements in disease states (increased protein needs in critical illness, surgery, and wounds), micronutrient deficiencies with specific clinical syndromes (Vitamin B12, folate, thiamine/Wernicke, Vitamin C/scurvy, iron-deficiency anemia, Vitamin D), nutritional support options in clinical practice (oral supplementation, enteral vs. parenteral nutrition — when to use each, advantages and risks), nutrition in specific diseases (chronic kidney disease — protein and electrolyte restriction, heart failure — sodium and fluid, diabetes — carbohydrate awareness, celiac disease — gluten), and how to document nutritional assessment and plan in clinical notes. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","nutrition","clinical-guidelines","hospital-medicine"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:05:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- SPECIALTY CLINICAL EDUCATION (89-100) ---
    {"id":"med-89","title":"Pediatric Clinical Assessment Teaching Guide","prompt":"You are a pediatrics educator writing a guide for medical students and residents on the unique aspects of clinical assessment in children. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the developmental approach to history-taking (adapting questions and communication to the child's age — infant, toddler, school-age, adolescent), involving parents and caregivers while still establishing rapport with the child, the Pediatric Assessment Triangle (general appearance, work of breathing, circulation to skin) as a rapid tool, vital sign interpretation by age (normal ranges differ significantly from adults — include a reference table for heart rate, respiratory rate, blood pressure, and temperature by age group), age-appropriate developmental milestones as context for clinical assessment, red flags in pediatric presentations (signs of serious bacterial infection in infants under 3 months, non-accidental injury pattern recognition, signs of sepsis in children), the unique challenges of pain assessment in pre-verbal children, and how to document a complete pediatric well-child visit. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","pediatrics","medical-education","clinical-skills"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-90","title":"Psychiatry Assessment Teaching Module","prompt":"You are a psychiatry educator writing a teaching guide for medical students and non-psychiatry residents on conducting a basic psychiatric assessment. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the components of the psychiatric history (chief complaint, history of present illness, past psychiatric history including hospitalizations and medication trials, substance use history, trauma history, family psychiatric history, social and developmental history, forensic history), the Mental Status Examination in detail (appearance, behavior, speech, mood vs. affect, thought process vs. thought content, perceptual disturbances, cognition, insight and judgment), how to assess suicidal ideation systematically (the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale framework — ideation, plan, intent, means, history, protective factors), how to assess homicidal ideation and the duty to warn, common psychiatric diagnoses explained in terms of their core clinical features for non-psychiatry clinicians (major depressive disorder, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia, GAD, PTSD, personality disorders), and when and how to refer for psychiatric consultation. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","psychiatry","medical-education","clinical-assessment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-91","title":"Obstetrics and Prenatal Care Education Guide","prompt":"You are an obstetrics educator writing a guide for medical students and family medicine residents on prenatal care fundamentals. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the components and schedule of routine prenatal care (initial visit, interval visits at recommended gestational ages, routine testing at each visit), standard first-trimester assessments (dating ultrasound, nuchal translucency, cell-free fetal DNA, first trimester labs — CBC, blood type and screen, rubella, syphilis, hepatitis B and C, HIV, Chlamydia, TB), second-trimester milestones (anatomy ultrasound at 18-20 weeks, quad screen, gestational diabetes screening at 24-28 weeks), Group B Streptococcus screening, Rh sensitization prevention, management of common pregnancy symptoms vs. red flag symptoms (preeclampsia warning signs, preterm labor signs), documentation requirements in obstetric records, and how to calculate gestational age and estimated due date from last menstrual period and ultrasound. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","obstetrics","prenatal-care","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-92","title":"Emergency Medicine Triage and Prioritization Framework","prompt":"You are an emergency medicine educator writing a teaching guide on triage and patient prioritization for medical students and residents beginning emergency medicine rotations. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the purpose and principles of triage (rapid sorting to allocate resources, not definitive diagnosis), the Emergency Severity Index (ESI) five-level triage system (levels 1-5, with criteria and examples for each level), time-sensitive emergencies that must never wait regardless of presentation (STEMI, stroke — time-to-treatment metrics, sepsis — hour-1 bundle, tension pneumothorax, ectopic pregnancy with hemodynamic instability), the concept of the sick vs. not-sick gestalt and how experienced clinicians develop it, how to reassess a patient whose condition changes while waiting, mass casualty incident START triage (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment), documentation in emergency triage, and communication between triage nurse and treating team. Include five fictional brief patient presentations rated by ESI level with teaching explanation. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","emergency-medicine","triage","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:35:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-93","title":"Geriatric Assessment and Frailty Education","prompt":"You are a geriatrics educator writing a comprehensive guide for medical students and residents on the principles of geriatric assessment. Write a guide covering: why geriatric patients are physiologically distinct from younger adults (altered pharmacokinetics, atypical disease presentation, the role of functional status over diagnosis, polypharmacy risk), the Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA) domains — functional status (ADLs and IADLs), cognitive assessment (Mini-Cog, MMSE, MoCA), mood and depression screening (GDS), social support and environment, nutritional status, vision and hearing, falls risk (Timed Up and Go test), frailty (Fried frailty phenotype and Clinical Frailty Scale), and polypharmacy review (Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medications in older adults), the concept of frailty as a risk stratification tool for surgery and acute illness, delirium prevention and management in hospitalized older adults, goals of care considerations in the context of frailty, and how to document a geriatric assessment. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","geriatrics","clinical-assessment","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-94","title":"Oncology Staging and Treatment Principles Education","prompt":"You are an oncology educator writing a teaching guide for medical students and internal medicine residents on the fundamentals of oncology staging and treatment principles. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the purpose and principles of cancer staging (anatomical extent of disease guides treatment selection and prognosis), the TNM staging system explained in detail (T = primary tumor size/extent, N = regional lymph node involvement, M = distant metastasis — how they combine into overall stage I-IV), the clinical vs. pathological staging distinction and when each applies, general treatment modality principles (surgery — curative intent vs. palliative, radiation therapy — external beam vs. brachytherapy, systemic therapy — cytotoxic chemotherapy mechanisms and major toxicities, targeted therapy — kinase inhibitors and their targets, immunotherapy — checkpoint inhibitors mechanism and immune-related adverse events), performance status scales (ECOG/WHO and Karnofsky — how they influence treatment decisions), the concept of neoadjuvant vs. adjuvant therapy, and how to discuss a new cancer diagnosis and treatment plan in a patient-centered way. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","oncology","medical-education","cancer-staging"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-95","title":"Critical Care and ICU Fundamentals for Trainees","prompt":"You are a critical care educator writing a guide for medical students and non-intensivist residents on ICU fundamentals. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the types of ICUs and general admission criteria, hemodynamic monitoring basics (arterial lines, central venous pressure, pulmonary artery catheters — what each measures and its limitations, interpretation of hemodynamic profiles), mechanical ventilation fundamentals for the non-intensivist (indications for intubation, basic ventilator settings — mode, tidal volume, PEEP, FiO2, respiratory rate, pressure support, lung-protective ventilation strategy in ARDS), vasopressor and inotrope selection principles (norepinephrine, dopamine, vasopressin, epinephrine, dobutamine — mechanism and clinical use), the sepsis bundle and Surviving Sepsis Campaign key recommendations, fluid resuscitation principles in critical illness (crystalloid vs. colloid, dynamic fluid responsiveness assessment), nutrition in the ICU, ICU documentation requirements (daily assessment of ventilator weaning readiness, sedation goals, delirium monitoring, DVT and stress ulcer prophylaxis), and family communication in the ICU. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","critical-care","icu","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:50:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"med-96","title":"Nephrology and AKI Education for Generalists","prompt":"You are a nephrology educator writing a guide for internal medicine residents on the evaluation and management principles of acute kidney injury (AKI). Write a comprehensive guide covering: AKI definition and KDIGO staging criteria (serum creatinine changes and urine output criteria), the pre-renal / intrinsic renal / post-renal classification framework and how to use history, physical exam, and basic laboratory data to differentiate them, the fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) and its appropriate interpretation and limitations, how to approach the urinalysis in AKI (casts and what they indicate — muddy brown granular casts, RBC casts, WBC casts), specific intrinsic AKI syndromes and their clinical features (ATN, contrast nephropathy, rhabdomyolysis, glomerulonephritis, interstitial nephritis), when to consider renal replacement therapy (indications including the AEIOU mnemonic), medication dose adjustment in AKI, how to write a comprehensive renal assessment note, and indications and urgency for nephrology consultation. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","nephrology","acute-kidney-injury","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-97","title":"Endocrinology and Diabetes Management Education","prompt":"You are an endocrinology educator writing a teaching guide for primary care residents and medical students on diabetes mellitus management principles. Write a comprehensive guide covering: distinguishing type 1, type 2, LADA, and MODY diabetes and their management implications, glycemic targets and how to individualize them (HbA1c goals differ by age, hypoglycemia risk, and patient preference), the major antidiabetic drug classes with mechanisms, benefits beyond glycemia, and key side effects (metformin, SGLT-2 inhibitors, GLP-1 receptor agonists, DPP-4 inhibitors, sulfonylureas, insulin — each class merits a paragraph), insulin types and regimens explained (basal, bolus, correction, basal-bolus strategy, how to start and titrate basal insulin in primary care), monitoring — self-monitoring of blood glucose vs. continuous glucose monitoring, managing hypoglycemia (recognition, treatment, documentation, prevention), managing hyperglycemia in the hospital (insulin infusion vs. subcutaneous protocols), diabetes complication screening schedule (eyes, kidneys, feet, cardiovascular risk), and patient education priorities for newly diagnosed diabetes. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","endocrinology","diabetes","medical-education"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T19:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-98","title":"Cardiovascular Risk Reduction Education for Clinicians","prompt":"You are a preventive cardiology educator writing a guide for primary care physicians on evidence-based cardiovascular risk reduction. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the major modifiable cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, dyslipidemia, diabetes, smoking, obesity, physical inactivity, diet) and relative magnitudes of risk, global cardiovascular risk assessment tools (Pooled Cohort Equations, SCORE2, HEART score for acute chest pain) and how to use them in clinical decisions, hypertension management principles (JNC/ACC/AHA guidelines, initial medication selection by compelling indication, resistant hypertension approach, white coat and masked hypertension), dyslipidemia management (LDL targets by risk category, statin therapy intensity classification, role of ezetimibe and PCSK9 inhibitors, when to treat triglycerides), antiplatelet therapy — primary vs. secondary prevention evidence and how it differs, lifestyle modification counseling (dietary patterns with cardiovascular evidence — Mediterranean and DASH, physical activity recommendations, smoking cessation benefit), and multimorbidity in cardiovascular patients. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","cardiology","preventive-medicine","clinical-guidelines"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T19:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-99","title":"Review a Clinical Document and Suggest Improvements","prompt":"You are a senior clinician, educator, and medical writing expert. Conduct a comprehensive expert review of the following clinical document. Your review should address: (1) Medical accuracy — identify any statements that appear inaccurate, outdated, or inconsistent with current evidence-based practice, citing specific concerns; (2) Completeness — identify critical information that is missing and should be added for the document to be clinically safe and complete; (3) Clarity and communication — flag ambiguous language, unexplained medical jargon, or sections where meaning is unclear; (4) Logical structure and flow — assess whether the document is organized in a way that serves its clinical purpose; (5) Tone appropriateness — is the tone suited to the intended audience (clinicians, patients, trainees, policymakers)? (6) Safety concerns — highlight any documentation that could create a patient safety risk if misinterpreted; (7) Specific line-by-line edits for the five highest-priority improvements. Conclude with an overall assessment and a revised version of the weakest section. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.\n\n[Paste your clinical document for expert review]","tags":["medicine","clinical-documentation","medical-writing","quality-improvement"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T19:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"med-100","title":"AI in Healthcare — Education and Critical Literacy Guide","prompt":"You are a clinical informatics and medical education specialist writing a guide for clinicians, educators, and students on the responsible use of artificial intelligence tools in healthcare and medical education. Write a comprehensive guide covering: the types of AI applications currently in or entering clinical practice (clinical decision support, diagnostic imaging AI, NLP for documentation, predictive analytics, remote monitoring), how to critically evaluate an AI tool for clinical use (training data characteristics, validation methodology, performance metrics — sensitivity, specificity, AUC, calibration, subgroup performance, prospective vs. retrospective validation), the known limitations and failure modes of medical AI (distribution shift, underrepresentation of minorities in training data, AI overreliance and automation bias, lack of explainability), regulatory pathways for medical AI devices (FDA Software as a Medical Device framework), using AI tools in medical education — opportunities (personalized learning, simulated patients, feedback on documentation) and risks (critical thinking atrophy, overreliance, academic integrity), the ethical framework for AI in medicine (accountability, transparency, fairness, patient autonomy, data privacy), and guidelines for responsible AI-assisted clinical documentation. For educational purposes — clinical decisions require qualified medical professionals.","tags":["medicine","medical-ai","medical-education","clinical-informatics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T19:15:00Z","featured":true}

];
